<h2 id="test-on-your-badge">4. Test on your badge</h2>

<p>
  Wait until Copilot says copying has finished. The skill can eject BADGER and
  wait for the badge to restart into its menu. Follow Copilot's test steps:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Open your app from the badge menu. If the menu has not returned after ejecting, press <strong>RESET</strong> once.</li>
  <li>Try its screens and buttons, then press <strong>HOME</strong> to return to the menu.</li>
  <li>If the app saves data, reopen it and check the data after a reset too.</li>
</ol>

<p>
  Copilot can check files and copy them, but it needs you to report what the
  badge displays and how the controls work. Sensors and wireless features need
  tests on the real badge; passing file checks does not prove they work.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>If you need to eject by hand,</strong> wait until copying has finished,
  then use the steps for your computer:
</p>

<p>
  <strong class="font-mono">Windows:</strong> press <strong>Windows key + E</strong>
  to open File Explorer. Under <strong>This PC</strong>, right-click
  <strong>BADGER</strong> and choose <strong>Eject</strong>. Its drive letter may
  differ between computers.
</p>

<p>
  <strong class="font-mono">macOS:</strong> open Finder and click the
  <strong>Eject</strong> button beside <strong>BADGER</strong> under
  <strong>Locations</strong>. If external disks are hidden, enable them in
  <strong>Finder &gt; Settings &gt; Sidebar</strong>.
</p>

<p>
  Wait for BADGER to disappear. If it is still present and your computer says it
  is in use, close files or terminals using the badge and try again; do not
  force eject. The badge normally returns to its menu by itself. Press
  <strong>RESET</strong> once if it does not.
</p>
